  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "jkv9pJKUj7NEhDKe8kzECVWc130=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:49.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`onOperations` receives a flat list that can be arbitrarily long \u2014 a backfill over a drive's full history arrives page by page, not operation by operation. Writing one row per operation turns that into one database round-trip per operation. Both shipped analytics processors use the same buffer-and-chunk shape, and the migration guide names it as the recommended pattern:\n\n```typescript\nconst CHUNK_SIZE = 50;\nconst buffer: AnalyticsSeriesInput[] = [];\n\nfor (const { operation, context } of operations) {\n  // ...build seriesInput...\n  buffer.push(seriesInput);\n\n  while (buffer.length >= CHUNK_SIZE) {\n    const batch = buffer.splice(0, CHUNK_SIZE);\n    await this.analyticsStore.addSeriesValues(batch);\n  }\n}\n\nif (buffer.length > 0) {\n  await this.analyticsStore.addSeriesValues(buffer);\n}\n```\n\nTwo details are easy to get wrong. The flush uses `while`, not `if` \u2014 with `splice` draining the front, `while` is correct for a buffer that could already be over the threshold, and it keeps the invariant \"never hold more than CHUNK_SIZE-1 items\". And the trailing `if (buffer.length > 0)` is not optional: without it every partial final chunk is silently dropped.\n\n`IAnalyticsStore` exposes both `addSeriesValue(input)` and `addSeriesValues(inputs)`. The chunked pattern uses the plural form; the singular is a convenience for one-off writes and is what the older Analytics Engine prose shows.\n\nBecause a processor registered with `startFrom: \"beginning\"` can be re-delivered its entire history \u2014 on first registration against a drive with history, after a restart gap, or after `tracked.retry()` \u2014 chunked inserts alone are not idempotent. The `clearSource` companion pattern handles that: on seeing operation index 0 again for a known document, call `clearSeriesBySource(source, true)` and rebuild, so reprocessing converges on the same totals.\n\nProvenance: academy `04-Reference/04-Processors/08-ProcessorMigrationGuide.md` (published) and the draft `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_04-analytics-processor.md`; code verified byte-for-byte in `packages/shared/processors/drive-analytics/{drive,document}-processor.ts` at 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:34:49.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:49.000Z"
    }
  },
